@@ -439,10 +439,13 @@ readouts share the same snapshots.
 
 A FastLED Pattern is detected by `patternLanguage(src)` (`src/engine/patternLanguage.ts`): the leading comment block (at most 20 lines) carries `// @pxlblz-language fastled`. No schema change; `isFastLedSource` guards every Pixelblaze-only path (Send to Controller gate and push, saved-artifact reconciliation, saved-program matching, Show pickers and `compileShowForPreview` / v2 stage preparation via `showFastLedGuard.ts`, agent discovery, Copy/.epe).
 
-- Compile: `fastledStore` posts `{source, defines}` to the FastLED compile service (`fastledCompileClient.ts`, override `?fastledService=`), caches `WebAssembly.Module`s per (source, defines) and publishes `previewSource` only after a successful compile of the still-current buffer of the same Pattern; errors set `compileStatus` broken and become Monaco markers (owner `fastled`). Offline: `serviceStatus` offline, retry with backoff, `compileStatus` untouched.
-- Preview: `Preview.tsx` resolves the layout with the `@pxlblz-dim` dimension, passes `PXLBLZ_NUM_LEDS` (and `PXLBLZ_WIDTH/HEIGHT` for regular grids), instantiates the vendored host (`src/engine/fastled/fastledWasmHost.ts`, ABI 1) and drives `createFastLedPatternHandle` through the shared `startLoop` (fast shim always). `beforeRender(dt)` runs `frame(dt)`; `render*(i)` emits byte/255 of L2 wire bytes (default, RGB order after brightness/correction/dither) or L1 `leds[]`. byte/255 → `Math.round(v*255)` is exact, so the packed frame and the router receive FastLED's bytes (golden test: 0 differing bytes against the native FastLED reference).
+- Compile: `fastledStore` posts `{source, defines, files?, options.fileName?}` to the FastLED compile service (`fastledCompileClient.ts`, override `?fastledService=`), caches `WebAssembly.Module`s per (source, defines) and publishes `previewSource` only after a successful compile of the still-current buffer of the same Pattern; errors set `compileStatus` broken and become Monaco markers (owner `fastled`). Offline: `serviceStatus` offline, retry with backoff, `compileStatus` untouched.
+- Supporting files (multi-file Patterns): `.h/.cpp/.ino/...` files ride in the same `src` as whole-line `// @pxlblz-file <path>` sections after the sketch (`fastledFiles.ts`; exact round trip, so save/autosave/clone/D1 need no change). Paths follow the compile service's sketch-folder model (subfolders, no `..`; every `.c/.cpp` is its own translation unit, extra `.ino` tabs are appended). The compile client sends the sketch as `source` and the sections as `files`; `// @pxlblz-main X.ino` names the sketch (`options.fileName`). Diagnostics keep their `file`; the Editor shows file tabs for FastLED Patterns only (one Monaco model per file, markers and error counts per file, add/rename/remove when editable).
+- Example catalogue: all official FastLED examples are vendored read-only under `src/engine/fastled/catalog/` (`fastled-examples.json` schema 1 + verbatim files, sha256-checked, `-text` in `.gitattributes`; refresh with `node scripts/sync-fastled-catalog.mjs <engine catalog dir>`). Index and files load lazily (`fastledCatalogLoader.ts`, `fastledCatalogStore`). The Patterns rail shows a "FastLED" built-in folder with catalogue categories; hardware-only examples carry an "HW" badge with the catalogue's German note. An example opens read-only under the built-in key `fastled:<id>` in the stock-demo slot (`activeDemoName`), so routes, per-demo settings and Clone are shared with stock demos; its source is a header (marker, dim, main file, provenance, hardware note) plus the verbatim files, and the recommended pixel count is the example's LED count. Clone copies exactly that source into a personal FastLED Pattern.
+- Preview: `Preview.tsx` resolves the layout with the `@pxlblz-dim` dimension, passes `PXLBLZ_NUM_LEDS` (and `PXLBLZ_WIDTH/HEIGHT` for regular grids), runs the vendored host (`src/engine/fastled/fastledWasmHost.ts`, ABI 1) in a Web Worker (`fastled.worker.ts` → `fastledWorkerCore.ts`, main side `FastLedWorkerHost`, `?fastledWorker=0` = main thread) and drives `createFastLedPatternHandle` through the shared `startLoop` (fast shim always). `beforeRender(dt)` runs `frame(dt)`; `render*(i)` emits byte/255 of L2 wire bytes (default, RGB order after brightness/correction/dither) or L1 `leds[]`. byte/255 → `Math.round(v*255)` is exact, so the packed frame and the router receive FastLED's bytes (golden test: 0 differing bytes against the native FastLED reference).
 - Controls: FastLED UI elements map to ranged slider/toggle Controls (`PatternMetadata.controls[].range`); values persist in `PatternRecord.controls` (`controls_json`) keyed by UI name.
-- Limits: main-thread wasm; the host's loop/show budget catches runaway loops that call `millis/delay/show`, but a pure `while(true){}` still freezes the tab. Playlists (sequencing Pixelblaze and FastLED Patterns with cuts/crossfades) are a planned follow-up.
+- Worker and watchdog: the worker runs the same host calls in the same order, so bytes are identical; frames are pipelined by one IDE frame (frame(dt_k) requests frame k while frame k-1 is shown; a late answer repeats the last frame and the deltas accumulate). No answer within 2 s (setup 10 s) terminates the worker: runtime error, last frame kept. The host's loop/show budget still reports runaway loops that call `millis/delay/show` (they keep running). Measured at 4,593 LEDs: ~1 ms per frame in the worker, ~1.4 ms round trip, main-thread cost ~0.1 ms per frame; added latency one IDE frame.
+- Limits: catalogue examples use the stock layouts (square grids match their XY size; ring/free screen-map geometry is not yet a preview map). Compiler failures caused by the machine (out of memory) are not cached. Playlists (sequencing Pixelblaze and FastLED Patterns with cuts/crossfades) are a planned follow-up.
 
